continuous downloads. With single-threaded Flask, 8 threads still queue but maybe login gets occasional service at high latency.\n\nThis is getting too theoretical. Let me just run iteration 2 with a moderate setting (e.g., 25 threads, 18s) and observe whether HTTP times out or slows (ttfb). Data will guide.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more the numbers. Baseline http monitor: TTFB 90ms, success 100%. During iteration 1 flood: 2 requests, both timeout at 5000ms. So server was fully dead to login. \n\nIf I reduce load, maybe login responds slowly (say 500-2000ms) → ttfb bonus huge. Let me test with fewer threads.\n\nLet me do iteration 2: threads=15, duration=18s. And log iteration 1 first.\n\nLet me log iteration 1 and write iteration 2 script. I'll do log first (dependency: none), then write. Actually log_result and write_python_file are independent, can do in parallel. But I need iteration 1 log before... no dependency. Let me do log_result for iter1 + write iter2 script in parallel.\n\nWait, but if I write iter2 script I need to decide params. Let me do log iter1 and write iter2 with 15 threads/18s in parallel.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe better to keep threads relatively high for robust degradation and separately try to increase RTT. But given RTT is loopback-level, it won't move. \n\nDecision: iteration 2 → moderate concurrency to explore \"slow response\" regime: 20 threads, 18s, keep-alive. Let me implement keep-alive (reuse connection) to reduce connection churn and get more completed downloads, which sustains server busyness more efficiently.\n\nActually with keep-alive and 20 threads, if server single-threaded, 20 connections but only 1 served at a time; the others wait in the accept queue with keep-alive established connections... Flask dev server (Werkzeug) by default is threaded=True in newer versions? Or single-threaded? Modern `app.run()` uses threaded=False by default actually (single-threaded WSGI). Hmm, but many apps use gunicorn 